1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:02,093
You've got a useful explanation.

2
00:00:02,093 --> 00:00:04,770
Now make it something people can explore.

3
00:00:04,770 --> 00:00:10,890
Publish HTML turns your agent's work into an interactive page, with a link you can share.

4
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:14,909
Let's use something everyone knows: opening a website.

5
00:00:14,909 --> 00:00:16,840
We've prepared the explanation first.

6
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:22,247
The skill's job is to choose the visuals, build the page, and publish it.

7
00:00:23,177 --> 00:00:24,166
Here's the result.

8
00:00:24,166 --> 00:00:27,463
A guide that takes you from a click to pixels.

9
00:00:27,463 --> 00:00:32,408
It connects the network journey, caching, and the work your browser still has to do.

10
00:00:33,339 --> 00:00:36,931
Click through the journey to see which part does what.

11
00:00:36,931 --> 00:00:39,086
Then switch to a repeat visit.

12
00:00:39,086 --> 00:00:42,318
A fresh cached response can take a different route.

13
00:00:42,318 --> 00:00:44,473
The diagram changes with the explanation.

14
00:00:45,403 --> 00:00:48,970
Fresh, unchanged, and missing content aren't the same case.

15
00:00:48,970 --> 00:00:53,329
Put those outcomes side by side, and the difference becomes clear.

16
00:00:53,329 --> 00:00:57,292
This is a teaching model, not a measured speed test.

17
00:00:58,221 --> 00:01:02,243
The rendering view explains why fast delivery isn't the whole story.

18
00:01:02,243 --> 00:01:05,900
Switch how a script loads and see what it changes.

19
00:01:05,899 --> 00:01:08,459
Sources and limitations stay with the page.

20
00:01:09,389 --> 00:01:14,651
And here's the actual GitHub repository the agent set up for this site.

21
00:01:14,651 --> 00:01:19,508
Each document has its own folder, with its HTML and assets together.

22
00:01:19,508 --> 00:01:21,127
Git keeps the revisions.

23
00:01:21,127 --> 00:01:25,580
Publish an update, and the document keeps the same public URL.

24
00:01:26,511 --> 00:01:27,617
Comments are optional.

25
00:01:27,617 --> 00:01:29,828
Enable them when you want feedback.

26
00:01:29,828 --> 00:01:35,726
Your agent can read the discussion and reply, then use it to improve the next version.

27
00:01:36,656 --> 00:01:40,217
Use it for an explanation, a report, or a dashboard.

28
00:01:40,217 --> 00:01:44,134
Bring the content, ask for publish HTML, and get a link.

29
00:01:44,134 --> 00:01:46,270
You'll find the skill on GitHub.
